<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Eastern Wu</h1><p class="st-meta">222 CE – 280 CE · capital Jianye</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Three Kingdoms state of the Sun family in the lower Yangtze, which held the south with river fleets for six decades and laid the base of Nanjing as a capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-ce">Sun Ce</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-jian">Sun Jian</a>, a general against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion">Yellow Turbans</a>, conquered the region south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> in the 190s. Sun Ce's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-red-cliffs">Red Cliffs</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> in 208, took Jing province from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a> in 219 and beat Liu Bei at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-xiaoting">Yiling</a> in 222. He declared himself emperor in 229, with his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jianye</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Wu relied on powerful southern clans and large navies, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-colony">military colonies</a> developing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> delta and the far south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> sent fleets to the island of Yizhou and envoys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-ying">Zhu Ying</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-tai">Kang Tai</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/funan">Funan</a> in South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Northern refugees and settlers spread farming southwards.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan's</a> death in 252, succession struggles weakened the state, and the last emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-hao">Sun Hao</a>, became notorious for cruelty. In 280 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-jin">Western Jin</a> fleets descended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and Sun Hao surrendered, reuniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eastern-wu.jpg" alt="Eastern Wu" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Kingdoms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>222 CE – 280 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jianye</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sun Quan, Sun Liang, Sun Hao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wu (Three Kingdoms), Sun Wu</dd></dl></aside></div>

